Q: In &quot;.NET MAUI Blazor Hybrid and Web App&quot;-type project, got &quot;An unhandled error has occurred&quot; in GUI but to error output anywhere A: <p>As it clear from the <strong>MainLayout.razor</strong> file, the element containing &quot;An unhandled error has occurred&quot; message is <em>not</em> been <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/razor?view=aspnetcore-10.0#conditionals-if-else-if-else-and-switch" rel="nofollow noreferrer">conditionally rendered</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>@inherits LayoutComponentBase

@Body

&lt;div id=&quot;blazor-error-ui&quot; data-nosnippet&gt;
    An unhandled error has occurred.
    &lt;a href=&quot;.&quot; class=&quot;reload&quot;&gt;Reload&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;span class=&quot;dismiss&quot;&gt;🗙&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Instead, the manipulations with dynamic CSS styles are been used.
Initially, the element with ID <code>blazor-error-ui</code> hidden with <code>display: none</code>.
This ruleset declared in <strong>MainLayout.razor.css</strong>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#blazor-error-ui {
    color-scheme: light only;
    background: lightyellow;
    bottom: 0;
    box-shadow: 0 -1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
    box-sizing: border-box;
    display: none;
    left: 0;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem 0.7rem 1.25rem;
    position: fixed;
    width: 100%;
    z-index: 1000;
}

    #blazor-error-ui .dismiss {
        cursor: pointer;
        position: absolute;
        right: 0.75rem;
        top: 0.5rem;
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Thus, if for some reason the page will loose the access to this stylesheet, the message will displayed. That is exactly what happen in above case.</p>
<p>Thanks to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/2658697/mister-magoo">Mister Magoo</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/6643940/h-a-h">H.A.H.</a> for the reaching to this cause.</p>

Q: `reinterpret_cast` between unrelated structs with the same layout A: <p>Although certainly not by any means best practices, there are exceptional cases where two orthogonal objects need to occupy the same space.</p>
<p>First, let me answer your question, then i will explain why this is such a baaaad idea.</p>
<p>There are several ways you can go about this <em>without</em> any casting.</p>
<p>Option A: Use an anonymous union</p>
<pre><code>struct A
{
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct B
{
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct S
{
    union
    {
        A a;
        B b;
    };
};
</code></pre>
<p>Usage:</p>
<pre><code>S s{};  

s.a.x = 10;  
s.a.y = 2.5f;  
s.a.z = 9.0;  
s.a.arr\[0\] = 1;
</code></pre>
<p>To test this:</p>
<pre><code>
#include &lt;cassert&gt;

U u{};

u.a.x = 1;
u.a.y = 2.0f;
u.a.z = 3.0;
u.a.arr[0] = 4;
u.a.arr[1] = 5;
u.a.arr[2] = 6;

assert(
    u.a.x == u.b.x &amp;&amp;
    u.a.y == u.b.y &amp;&amp;
    u.a.z == u.b.z &amp;&amp;
    u.a.arr[0] == u.b.arr[0] &amp;&amp;
    u.a.arr[1] == u.b.arr[1] &amp;&amp;
    u.a.arr[2] == u.b.arr[2]
);
</code></pre>
<p>Option B: Two interfaces over the same data at the same time</p>
<p>Maybe what your after is not two orthogonal objects sitting on top of each other, but rather two interfaces that are looking at the same state. In that case, make one storage object and have two wrappers/views refer to it:</p>
<pre><code>struct SharedData
{
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct AView
{
    SharedData&amp; d;

    int&amp; x()       { return d.x; }
    float&amp; y()     { return d.y; }
    double&amp; z()    { return d.z; }
    int&amp; arr(int i){ return d.arr[i]; }
};

struct BView
{
    SharedData&amp; d;

    int&amp; x()       { return d.x; }
    float&amp; y()     { return d.y; }
    double&amp; z()    { return d.z; }
    int&amp; arr(int i){ return d.arr[i]; }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Option C: std::variant</p>
<p>You might want to adopt the policy of “one of these types lives here right now.”</p>
<p>This option uses shared internal storage, and also tracks which object is currently active. Your code might look like this::</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;variant&gt;

struct A {
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct B {
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

using V = std::variant&lt;A, B&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>Your usage would be:</p>
<pre><code>V v = A{1, 2.0f, 3.0, {4, 5, 6}};
</code></pre>
<p>Option D: Polymorphism</p>
<p>Presumably you have access to both types in some header file. You could just derive A and B from a base interface class:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct IThing
{
    virtual ~IThing() = default;
    virtual void Dump(std::ostream&amp; os) const = 0;
};

struct A : public IThing
{
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];

    void Dump(std::ostream&amp; os) const override
    {
        os &lt;&lt; &quot;A { &quot;
           &lt;&lt; &quot;x=&quot; &lt;&lt; x
           &lt;&lt; &quot;, y=&quot; &lt;&lt; y
           &lt;&lt; &quot;, z=&quot; &lt;&lt; z
           &lt;&lt; &quot;, arr=[&quot; &lt;&lt; arr[0] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; arr[1] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; arr[2] &lt;&lt; &quot;]&quot;
           &lt;&lt; &quot; }&quot;;
    }
};

struct B : public IThing
{
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];

    void Dump(std::ostream&amp; os) const override
    {
        os &lt;&lt; &quot;B { &quot;
           &lt;&lt; &quot;x=&quot; &lt;&lt; x
           &lt;&lt; &quot;, y=&quot; &lt;&lt; y
           &lt;&lt; &quot;, z=&quot; &lt;&lt; z
           &lt;&lt; &quot;, arr=[&quot; &lt;&lt; arr[0] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; arr[1] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; arr[2] &lt;&lt; &quot;]&quot;
           &lt;&lt; &quot; }&quot;;
    }
};

void PrintThing(const IThing&amp; thing)
{
    thing.Dump(std::cout);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\n';
}

int main()
{
    A a{ 1, 2.0f, 3.0, { 4, 5, 6 } };
    B b{ 10, 20.0f, 30.0, { 40, 50, 60 } };

    PrintThing(a);
    PrintThing(b);

    IThing* p = &amp;a;
    p-&gt;Dump(std::cout);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\n';

    p = &amp;b;
    p-&gt;Dump(std::cout);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\n';

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Option E: Shared storage</p>
<p>If you insist on using casts, then might want to use a byte buffer with alignment. At least this way, there is a bit more type safety when you cast:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cstddef&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;new&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

struct A {
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct B {
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct Storage
{
    alignas(std::max(alignof(A), alignof(B)))
    std::byte buf[std::max(sizeof(A), sizeof(B))];
};

int main()
{
    Storage s{};

    A* pa = std::construct_at(reinterpret_cast&lt;A*&gt;(s.buf));
    pa-&gt;x = 1;

    std::destroy_at(pa);

    B* pb = std::construct_at(reinterpret_cast&lt;B*&gt;(s.buf));
    pb-&gt;x = 2;

    std::destroy_at(pb);
}
</code></pre>
<p>OK, that's the how. Let's talk what kind of issues you would face beyond the wild west use of reinterpret_cast.</p>
<p>A. Structure alignment</p>
<p>Even if you use a #pragma directive, you still need to make sure that both objects start on an address that aligns with the underlying CPU/OS, and here is where you are likely to run into trouble.</p>
<p>Let's change A and B to include a char field:</p>
<pre><code>
struct A
{
    int x;
    float y;
    char q;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};
struct B
{
    int x;
    float y;
    char q;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

</code></pre>
<p>You now have to play memory address alignment tricks to get this to work and not fault the processor.</p>
<p>B: Endianness and type size</p>
<p>We are all presuming that you will be running this app on one machine. You are now either stuck with a brittle requirement to run on one machine (type) using one compiler and one OS version that cannot (easily) change.</p>
<p>C: Marshaling of data</p>
<p>If you do decide that these objects can live on the wire, you cannot guarantee that both A and B will look the same on the other side.</p>
<p>D: reinterpret_cast</p>
<p>It's important to remember that reinterpret_cast changes the <em>pointer type</em>, not the object.</p>
<p>This:</p>
<pre><code>struct A {
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

struct B {
    int x;
    float y;
    double z;
    int arr[3];
};

A* a = new A();
B* b = reinterpret_cast&lt;B*&gt;(a);
</code></pre>
<p>does <em>NOT</em> turn an A into a B.</p>
<p>After new A(), there is exactly one object there, and its type is A.
b is just a B* pointing at storage that contains an A, not a B.</p>
<p>Here's why this is bad:</p>
<ol>
<li>There is no B object</li>
</ol>
<p>This is <em>NOT</em> legitimate code:</p>
<pre><code>b-&gt;x = 123;
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>Dereferencing b results in undefined behavior</li>
</ol>
<p>Once you access the object through B*, you are telling the compiler “there is a B here”.</p>
<p>There is no B.</p>
<p>&quot;This is not the 'B' you are looking for&quot;</p>
<ol start="3">
<li>Your cast breaks compiler aliasing assumptions</li>
</ol>
<p>The C++ compiler is allowed to assume that A* and B* do (might) not refer to the same live object.</p>
<p>Code like this:</p>
<pre><code>void f(A* a, B* b)
{
    a-&gt;x = 10;
    b-&gt;x = 20;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; a-&gt;x &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre>
<p>will likely miscomppile.</p>
<ol start="4">
<li>This code is so, so fragile</li>
</ol>
<p>It only “looks okay” because today A and B happen to match. You're locked in forver.</p>
<p>But suppose another developer comes along and make a legitimate change that breaks this brittle contract. It might result in:</p>
<ul>
<li>different member order</li>
<li>added padding-sensitive member</li>
<li>different alignment</li>
<li>private/public layout changes</li>
<li>base class added</li>
<li>virtual function added</li>
<li>pragma pack differences</li>
<li>compiler/ABI differences</li>
</ul>
<p>Your fake B* will likely not even line up with the bytes the way you think.</p>
<p>And my favorite:</p>
<ol start="5">
<li>delete b;</li>
</ol>
<p>That is just so wrong on so many levels. Once this poison code is executed, all bets are off. You might be lucky and crash, or you might continue on for minutes, hours, days or weeks happily corrupting code or data. By the time you catch it (and good luck catching that), the damage is done.</p>
<p>Let's just cut to the chase... don't do that.</p>
<p>Also, ask yourself this question... if you are pursuing this as an answer, maybe you are asking the wrong question. ;)</p>
